<<data, include=FALSE>>=
### Loading data from "copd_redux.RData" file in the working directory:
load("./copd_redux.RData")
@

<<missingness>>=
n_obs <- nrow(copd) # Number of participants
n_vars <- ncol(copd) # Number of variables
nans <- 100*colMeans(is.na(copd)) # Percentage of missing data by variable
min_nan <- sprintf("%.2g", min(nans)) # MIRAR # Lower percentage
max_nan <- sprintf("%.2g", max(nans)) # Upper percentage

# Vairables with less than 5% of data missing (percentage)
less_5 <- sprintf("%.2g", 100*length(nans[nans<5])/n_vars) 

# Percentage of complete cases
comp_cases <- sprintf("%.2g", 100*mean(complete.cases(copd)))

# Percentage of missing data
miss_total <-  sprintf("%.2g", 100*mean(is.na(copd)))

# Total cells observed (variables*observations)
total_cells = prettyNum(n_obs*n_vars, big.mark = ",", decimal.mark = ".")
@

“The subset used here included \Sexpr{n_obs} participants and \Sexpr{n_vars} variables. The range of missing data in the different variables went from \Sexpr{min_nan}\% to \Sexpr{max_nan}\%, with \Sexpr{less_5}\% of the variables having less than 5\% of the data missing. Only \Sexpr{comp_cases}\% of the participants had complete data on all \Sexpr{n_vars} variables. Overall, \Sexpr{miss_total}\% of the information was missing; that is, \Sexpr{miss_total}\% of the total of \Sexpr{total_cells} cells (\Sexpr{n_vars} × \Sexpr{n_obs} = \Sexpr{total_cells}) had missing data.”

\subsection{Multiple imputation}

Apply multiple imputation to copd data using 100 imputations. Explain in a reasoned manner how you decided to set the values of the arguments \texttt{method} and \texttt{predictorMatrix} when using the \texttt{mice()} function. Show your code.

<<imputation_ini>>=

# Display of default imputation methods for each variable
ini <- mice(copd, maxit=0)
ini
@

We can see that the mice package suggest imputing initialy with de mean for continuous variables and with a logistic regression for binary ones. Further research on the dataset has shown that the variables have no deterministic relation. So we do not change the imputation methods. We have a variable (\texttt{id}) that don't have any role in the imputation process, so we change \texttt{predictorMatrix} and \texttt{methods} acording to that.

<<imputation_parameters>>=
# Change in parameters
meth <- ini$method
pred <- ini$predictorMatrix

meth["id"] <- "" # do not impute "id"
pred["id", ] <- 0 # we don't want "id" to be imputed
pred[, "id"] <- 0 # we don't want "id" to be predictor
@

<<imputation>>=
# Imputation of 100 datasets
M <- 100 # Number of imputations for each missing value
imp <- mice(data = copd, 
            m = M, 
            method = meth, 
            predictorMatrix = pred,
            maxit = 20,
            printFlag = FALSE,
            seed = 666)
# imp$imp (output llarg)
#summary(imp)
@


\subsection{Integration of multiple imputation in cluster analysis}

Reproduce the original analysis in the paper using the miclust package to integrate multiple
imputation in the cluster analysis. Apply the proposed framework using the same setting
than in the original analysis. To do that, follow these steps:

\begin{enumerate}
  \item Read the help of the \texttt{getdata()} function in the \texttt{miclust} package. Then, accordingly, 
  create a \textit{list} of \textit{data.frames} and pass it to the \texttt{getdata()} function. Give a detailed
  explanation of the data processing you did to get that \textit{list}. Show your code.
  
<<data_prep>>=
# ?miclust::getdata
# getdata() requires the data to be a list or data.frame where the first element is the raw data:
  
  #1- prepare raw data (all numeric and without id) 
copd_raw <- copd
copd_raw$id <- NULL
num_cols <- sapply(copd_raw, function(x) is.numeric(x) || is.integer(x) || is.logical(x))
copd_raw <- copd_raw[, num_cols]

  #2- prepare imputated data
imp_list <- complete(imp, action = "all") # extracts the completed data from a mids object
imp_list_clean <- lapply(imp_list, function(df) {
  df$id <- NULL
  df <- df[, num_cols]
  return(df)
})

  #3- create list where the first element is the raw data
my_cluster_list <- c(list(copd_raw), imp_list_clean)

# prepare the data for miclust
imp_midata <- miclust::getdata(data = my_cluster_list) 

# now we check
class(imp_midata)
length(imp_midata$impdata) 
@

The \texttt{getdata()} function needs a \texttt{data.frames} list where the first element contains the original data (with missing values) and the remaining ones correspond to multiple imputed datasets. That is why we created \texttt{my\_cluster\_list}, the list of data frames that we pass to \texttt{getdata()}. The imputed data sets were extracted from the \texttt{mids}
object, and the same processing was applied to the original data and to each imputed data set, so that all of them have the same variables in the same order.
Since k-means groups the subjects using Euclidean distances, we removed the variables that are neither numeric nor logical. We also removed the \texttt{id} variable, because it is just an identifier and does not contain any valuable clinical information. For the same reason, the \texttt{id} was not included as a predictor in the imputation.
  
  \item Use the \texttt{miclust()} function to perform the integration of missing data in cluster
  analysis using the same settings as in the original analysis. Show your code and
  explain how and why you set all arguments in the \texttt{miclust()} function.

<<cluster_analysis>>=
miclust_1 <- miclust(data = imp_midata, 
                     method = "kmeans", 
                     search = "backward",
                     ks = 2:5,
                     usedimp = 1:100,
                     seed = 666)

#summary(miclust_1)
miclust_1$kfin # optimal k (number of clusters)
@


<<cluster_analysis_text, include = FALSE>>=
# Number of subjects per candidate variable.
ratio <- sprintf("%.1f", nrow(copd_raw) / ncol(copd_raw))

# Sentence about the need for variable selection, depending on the ratio.
txt_ratio <- if (nrow(copd_raw) / ncol(copd_raw) < 10) {
  "< 10$, so a reduction of the number of variables is needed"
} else {
  "\\geq 10$, so a reduction is not strictly needed, but we apply it as in the original analysis"
}
@
  
  
We need to perform a variable selection because adding variables can degrade the classification when the number of subjects is small compared to the number of variables. As mentioned in the article, it is good practice that the number of subjects is at least 10 times the number of variables. The candidate
variables are the \Sexpr{ncol(copd_raw)} numeric variables of \texttt{copd}, after removing \texttt{id} and the \Sexpr{sum(!num_cols)} non-numeric variables. In our case, the ratio is
$\Sexpr{nrow(copd_raw)} / \Sexpr{ncol(copd_raw)} = \Sexpr{ratio} \Sexpr{txt_ratio}.

As in the original analysis, we use the backward search with CritCF criterion. It starts with all the variables and, at each step, removes the variable that when excluded gives the highest CritCF. This criterion allows to rank partitions with different numbers of clusters and different numbers of variables.
Finally, we define \texttt{k = 2:5} where the maximum value was fixed a priori, the same way as the article. Since the backward seach is repeated for each $k$ in each imputed data set, limiting the number of clusters reduces the computational cost and also keeps the resulting groups interpretable.



\end{enumerate}

\section{Results}

Using \texttt{plot()} and \texttt{summary()} methods in the \texttt{miclust} package, provide plots and tables for the results similar than those shown in the original paper. Also, show a barplot for the percentage of times each variable is selected among the imputed data sets, in decreasing order. Explain how you set all arguments in all functions and methods you use. Show your code.

<<results_values>>=
# Final number of clusters selected by miclust
kfin <- miclust_1$kfin

# Number of imputations
n_imp <- length(miclust_1$usedimp)
@

In this section, we reproduce the figures and tables of Basagaña et al. using the \texttt{plot()} and \texttt{summary()} methods of the \texttt{miclust} object, named \texttt{miclust\_1}. Following the instructions, we use one colour for all plots related to the number of clusters, dark magenta, and another for all plots related to variable selection, sky blue.
<<fig_critcf, fig.pos='H', fig.align='center', fig.width=8, fig.height=8, out.width='0.9\\textwidth', fig.cap=paste0("\\label{fig:critcf}Distribution of the CritCF criterion (higher values are preferred) by number of clusters ($k$), across the ", n_imp, " imputed data sets. Each box summarises ", n_imp, " values obtained with $k$-means and backward variable selection for $k=", min(miclust_1$ks), ",...,", max(miclust_1$ks), "$. The final number of clusters, $k=", kfin, "$, is the one selected in most imputed data sets.")>>=

# Distribution of the CritCF criterion across the imputed data sets, one box per
# explored number of clusters.
plot(miclust_1, metric = "critcf", col.critcf = "darkmagenta")
@

Figure \ref{fig:fig_critcf} corresponds to Figure 1 of the original paper. It is obtained with \texttt{plot(miclust\_1, metric = "critcf")}, the argument \texttt{metric = "critcf"} selects the boxplots of the CritCF criterion by numbers of clusters, with one value per imputed data set and per $k$. The argument \texttt{col.critcf = "darkmagenta"} makes de boxes in color.

<<fig_varsel, fig.pos='H', fig.align='center', fig.width=8, fig.height=6, out.width='0.9\\textwidth', fig.cap=paste0("\\label{fig:varsel}Variable selection frequency for $k=", miclust_1$kfin, "$ clusters. Each horizontal line shows the percentage of the ", length(miclust_1$usedimp), " imputed data sets in which a variable remained in the final set after the backward selection algorithm. Only variables selected in at least one data set are shown. The black horizontal lines summarise the number of variables selected per imputed data set: first and third quartiles (dotted), median (dashed) and mean (dash-dotted), positioned according to the ranked list of variables. Variable names are those of the \\texttt{copd} data set.")>>=

# Percentage of appearance of each variable (metric = "varsel") for k = kfin.
plot(miclust_1, k = kfin, metric = "varsel", col.varsel = "skyblue")
@

Figure \ref{fig:fig_varsel} corresponds to Figure 2 of the original paper. We use \texttt{metric = "varsel"} to show only, for each variable, the percentages of imputed data sets in which it remained in the final set of variables. With \texttt{k = kfin} we show the results for the optimal number of clusters, as in the paper, where only the data sets with the final $k$ are used for variable selection. The argument \texttt{col.varsel = "skyblue"} sets the color of the segments.

<<fig_barplot, fig.pos='H', fig.align='center', fig.width=8, fig.height=8, out.width='0.9\\textwidth', fig.cap=paste0("\\label{fig:barplot}Percentage of the ", length(miclust_1$usedimp), " imputed data sets in which each variable was selected by the backward algorithm for $k=", miclust_1$kfin, "$, in decreasing order. Only variables selected in at least one imputed data set are shown. Bar heights represent the percentage of selection.")>>=

# Frequency of selection of each variable across the imputed data sets.
y   <- getvariablesfrequency(miclust_1)

# Sort the variables by decreasing percentage of selection.
y_ord <- order(y$percfreq, decreasing = TRUE)
perc <- y$percfreq[y_ord]
name <- y$varnames[y_ord]

# Bars are drawn as thick vertical lines (type = "h", lwd = 20).
plot(perc, type = "h", main = "", xlab = "", ylab = "Percentage of times selected", 
     xlim = 0.5 + c(0, length(perc)), ylim = c(0, 100),
     lwd = 20, col = "skyblue", xaxt = "n")

# Variable names below each bar: las = 2 draws them vertically, and
# cex.axis = 0.45 reduces their size so that the long names fit.
axis(1, at = 1:length(name), labels = name, las = 2, cex.axis = 0.45)

# This code is adapted from the miclust README:
# https://cran.r-project.org/web/packages/miclust/readme/README.html
@

Figure~\ref{fig:barplot} shows the same selection percentages as Figure~\ref{fig:varsel}, as a barplot in decreasing order. The percentages are obtained with \texttt{getvariablesfrequency(miclust\_1)}, which returns the variable names (\texttt{varnames}) and the percentage of times each one was selected (\texttt{percfreq}). They are sorted with \texttt{order(..., decreasing = TRUE)}, applying the same ordering to the percentages and to the names so that each bar keeps its label. The bars are drawn with \texttt{plot(type = "h")} and a thick line (\texttt{lwd = 20}). The default x axis is removed (\texttt{xaxt = "n"}) and replaced by \texttt{axis()}, with vertical labels (\texttt{las = 2}) and reduced size (\texttt{cex.axis}) so that long names fit. The y axis is fixed to \texttt{ylim = c(0, 100)} because the values are percentages, and \texttt{col = "skyblue"} keeps the color used for variable selection. The code is adapted from the \texttt{miclust} README.


<<summaries>>=
# Number of clusters used for comparison.
#   kfin + 1, if kfin < max(miclust_1$ks)
#   kfin - 1 else.
if (kfin < max(miclust_1$ks)) {
  k_comp <- kfin + 1
} else {
  k_comp <- kfin - 1
}

# Set quantilevars = 0.5 so the numbers of final variables equals the median 
# number of variables selected across the imputed data sets.
summary_k <- summary(miclust_1, quantilevars = 0.5)
summary_kp1 <- summary(miclust_1, k =  k_comp, quantilevars = 0.5)
@

The tables are built from the \texttt{summary()} method. We call \texttt{summary(miclust\_1, quantilevars = 0.5)}, which uses the optimal number of clusters ($k=\Sexpr{kfin}$). The argument \texttt{quantilevars = 0.5} makes the number of final variables equal to the median number of variables selected across the imputed data sets, and the variables retained are the most frequently selected ones, which is the criterion proposed in step~3a of Table~1 of Basagaña et al. We also call \texttt{summary()} with \texttt{k = k\_comp} ($k=\Sexpr{k_comp}$) only for comparison. The value \texttt{k\_comp} is $k_{\text{fin}}+1$, unless $k_{\text{fin}}$ is the largest explored number of clusters ($\Sexpr{max(miclust_1$ks)}$), in which case we use $k_{\text{fin}}-1$.

<<table_alloc, results='asis'>>=
# Distribution of the assignment probabilities for each cluster,
# for k = kfin.
a_fin <- as.matrix(summary_k$allocationprobabilities)

# Same distribution, for k = k_comp.
a_comp <- as.matrix(summary_kp1$allocationprobabilities) 

# Order the blocks by increasing number of clusters.
if (k_comp > kfin) {
  k1 <- kfin
  a1 <- a_fin
  k2 <- k_comp
  a2 <- a_comp
} else {
  k1 <- k_comp
  a1 <- a_comp
  k2 <- kfin
  a2 <- a_fin
}

# Numeric part: an empty row before each k block, followed by the rows
# of the corresponding summary.
vals <- rbind(rep(NA, 5), a1, rep(NA, 5), a2) 

# Text part: one label per row.
labels <- c(paste0("$k=", k1, "$"),
            paste0("Cluster ", seq_len(nrow(a1))),
            paste0("$k=", k2, "$"),
            paste0("Cluster ", seq_len(nrow(a2)))) 

# Combine the text labels in the first column with the numeric values.
tab_alloc <- data.frame(labels, vals) 


# Create the xtable object. 'digits' has seven elements: the first
# specifies the automatic row names (not printed), the second specifies
# the text labels, and the remaining five specify the numeric columns.
# Define the caption and label.
alloc <- xtable(tab_alloc,
                digits = c(0, 0, 2, 2, 2, 2, 2),
                align = c("l", "l", "c", "c", "c", "c", "c"),
                caption = paste0(
                  "Distribution of the assignment frequencies of participants ",
                  "to each cluster across the ", length(miclust_1$usedimp),
                  " imputed data sets, for $k=", k1, "$ and $k=", k2, "$ ",
                  "(the final number of clusters is $k=", kfin, "$; ",
                  "the other value is shown for comparison only). ",
                  "For each participant, the assignment frequency for a cluster ",
                  "is the proportion of imputed data sets in which the participant ",
                  "was assigned to that cluster. ",
                  "Each row gives the minimum, first quartile, median, third ",
                  "quartile, and maximum of these frequencies among participants ",
                  "finally assigned to that cluster by majority vote."),
                label = "tab:alloc")

# Column names, centred and in bold; the first column is left blank.
colnames(alloc) <- c("","\\multicolumn{1}{c}{\\bf Minimum}", 
                         "\\multicolumn{1}{c}{\\bf First quartile}", 
                         "\\multicolumn{1}{c}{\\bf Median}", 
                         "\\multicolumn{1}{c}{\\bf Third quartile}", 
                         "\\multicolumn{1}{c}{\\bf Maximum}") 

# Print the table as LaTeX code.
print(alloc,
      table.placement = "ht",
      size = "footnotesize",
      include.rownames = FALSE, # Row names are not printed, labels are in columne 1
      include.colnames = TRUE,
      floating = TRUE,
      sanitize.text.function = force, 
      booktabs = TRUE) 
@

Table~\ref{tab:alloc} corresponds to Table~2 of the original paper. It uses the field \texttt{allocationprobabilities} of the summaries for $k=\Sexpr{min(kfin, k_comp)}$ and $k=\Sexpr{max(kfin, k_comp)}$, which gives the minimum, quartiles and maximum of the frequency of assignment of the participants to their final cluster over the \Sexpr{n_imp} imputed data sets. A block of rows is built for each $k$, always in increasing order of $k$, so the table adapts if the number of clusters changes.

<<table_description, results='asis'>>=
# Final selected variables and the cluster summary.
vars <- summary_k$selectedvariables
summary_cluster <- summary_k$summarybycluster 

# Percentage of missing values for each variable.
miss <- summary_cluster$`%miss.` 

# Imputed data means for each variable by cluster.
mean_imputed <- as.matrix(
  summary_cluster[, grep("^mean", names(summary_cluster))])

# Majority-vote cluster assignment for each participant.
cl <- summary_k$cluster 

# Empty matrix (variables x clusters) for the raw-data means, and empty vector 
# for the raw-data F values.
raw_means <- matrix(NA, nrow = length(vars), ncol = kfin)
F_raw <- vector(length = length(vars)) 

# Raw data: compute the F-statistic and mean for each selected variable 
# in each cluster.
for (i in 1:length(vars)) {
      # Values of the selected variable for all participants.
      x <- copd_raw[[vars[i]]]
      # Fit a linear model with cluster as the explanatory factor.
      fit <- lm(x ~ factor(cl)) 
      # Extract the F-statistic for the cluster effect from the first row of 
      # the ANOVA table.
      F_raw[i] <- anova(fit)$`F value`[1]
  # Mean per cluster, excluding NAs to avoid getting NA values. 
  for (j in 1:kfin){
    raw_means[i,j] <- mean(x[cl == j], na.rm = TRUE)
  }
}

# Cluster assignments for each participant in each imputed data set 
# (participants x imputed data sets), and the number of imputed data sets.
vector_clusters <- summary_k$clustervectors
n_imp <- ncol(vector_clusters)

# Empty vector for the F values of the imputed data.
F_imputed <- vector(length = length(vars))

# Imputed data: same as for the raw data, but repeated in each imputed data set
# using its own cluster assignment, the F values are then averaged.
for (i in 1:length(vars)){
  f_n_imp <- vector(length = n_imp)
  for (m in 1:n_imp){
    y <- imp_midata$impdata[[m]][[vars[i]]] 
    fit <- lm(y ~ factor(vector_clusters[, m]))
    f_n_imp[m] <- anova(fit)$`F value`[1]
  }
  F_imputed[i] <- mean(f_n_imp)
}

# Cluster sizes in the raw data and median cluster sizes across imputed data sets.
n_raw     <- as.vector(table(factor(cl, levels = seq_len(kfin))))
n_imp_med <- summary_k$clusterssize[, "size"]

# Minimum and maximum cluster size overs the imputed data sets, for the caption.
range_txt <- paste0("cluster ", seq_len(kfin), ": ",
                    summary_k$clusterssize[, "min."], "--",
                    summary_k$clusterssize[, "max."], collapse = "; ")

# Escape underscores in variable names for LaTeX.
labels <- gsub("_", "\\\\_", vars) 

# Create the final data frame, with variable labels followed by the numerical 
# results.
tab_desc <- data.frame(labels, miss, raw_means, F_raw, mean_imputed, F_imputed)

# Create the xtable object. The caption and the label are defined here.
desc <- xtable(tab_desc,
               digits = c(0, 0, 1, rep(2, kfin), 2, rep(2, kfin), 2),
               align  = c("l", "l", rep("c", 2 * kfin + 3)),
               caption = paste0(
                 "Mean values of the ", length(vars),
                 " selected variables by cluster ($k=", kfin, "$). ",
                 "Raw data: majority-vote cluster assignment, ",
                 "missing values excluded. ",
                 "Imputed data: means over the ", n_imp,
                 " imputed data sets. ",
                 "$n$: number of participants per cluster (raw data: ",
                 "majority-vote assignment, imputed data: means over ",
                 "the imputed data sets, with minimum--maximum ", 
                 range_txt, "). ",
                 "$F$: ratio of between cluster variance ",
                 "from a linear regression of each variable on cluster ",
                 "(imputed data: mean over the imputed data sets). ",
                 "\\% missing: percentage of participants with missing values."),
               label = "tab:desc")

# Two-level header, first row have Raw data and Imputed data, the second row have
# variable, % missing, clusters with their sizes, and F values.
head_top <- paste0(
            "& & \\multicolumn{", kfin + 1, "}{c}{\\bf Raw data} & ",
            "\\multicolumn{", kfin + 1, "}{c}{\\bf Imputed data} \\\\\n")
head_sub <- paste0(
            "\\bf Variable & \\bf \\% missing & ",
            paste0("\\bf Cl.~", 1:kfin, " ($n=", n_raw, "$)", collapse = " & "),
            " & \\bf $F$ & ",
            paste0("\\bf Cl.~", 1:kfin, " ($n=", n_imp_med, "$)", collapse = " & "),
            " & \\bf $F$ \\\\\n")

# Create the cmidrule lines.
cmidrules <- paste0("\\cmidrule(lr){3-", kfin + 3, "} ",
                    "\\cmidrule(lr){", kfin + 4, "-", 2 * kfin + 4, "}\n")

# Store the complete add.to.row structure in a variable.
head_structure <- list(pos=list(0),
                       command = paste0(head_top, cmidrules, head_sub))

# Print the table as LaTeX code.
print(desc,
      table.placement = "H",
      size = "footnotesize",
      include.rownames = FALSE,
      include.colnames = FALSE,           # header is written by add.to.row
      add.to.row = head_structure,
      floating = TRUE,
      sanitize.text.function = force,
      booktabs = TRUE)
@

Table~\ref{tab:desc} corresponds to Table~3 of the original paper and describes the \Sexpr{length(vars)} final variables by cluster. The percentage of missing values and the means for the imputed data come from the field \texttt{summarybycluster}, and the cluster sizes (median, minimum and maximum over the imputed data sets) from \texttt{clusterssize}. For the raw data, each participant is assigned to the cluster given by majority vote (\texttt{summary\_k\$cluster}) and the mean of each variable by cluster is computed with \texttt{mean(..., na.rm = TRUE)}, so that missing values are excluded. The $F$ values are obtained with \texttt{lm()} of each variable on the cluster, as a \texttt{factor}, so that the cluster numbers are treated as labels, and \texttt{anova()}, taking the $F$ of the first row. For the imputed data, this is repeated in each of the \Sexpr{n_imp} imputed data sets with its own cluster assignment (\texttt{clustervectors}), and the $F$ values are averaged. The article does not specify how to combine them, so the mean is our choice.


\section{Report}

Write a brief report (400 words maximum) describing the following issues, and citing tables and figures:
  
  \begin{itemize}
\item The final number of clusters and their sizes and uncertainty (cite your figure(s) and table(s)).

\item The agreement when relabeling the clusters.

\item The final subset of clustering variables and uncertainty.

\item The probability of assignment to each cluster and uncertainty.
\end{itemize}

<<report_values, include = FALSE>>=
  # Number of candidate variables, variables selected at least once, variables
  # in the final subset and variables selected in all the imputed data sets.
  var_cand    <- ncol(copd_raw)
var_once    <- sum(perc > 0)
var_sel     <- length(summary_k$selectedvariables)
var_sel_all <- sum(perc == 100)

# Selection frequency (%) of the least frequent final variable and of the
# next variable, which was not selected.
p_last <- sprintf("%.0f", perc[var_sel])
p_next <- sprintf("%.0f", perc[var_sel + 1])

# Median probability of assignment of each cluster.
med1 <- paste(sprintf("%.2f", a1[, 3]), collapse = " and ")

# Size of each cluster after after assignment
n_final <- table(factor(summary_k$cluster, levels = 1:kfin)) 

# Cohen's kappa of each imputed data set and its median.
kappa_vals <- summary_k$kappas 
kappa_med <- sprintf("%.2f", median(kappa_vals))

@
  
  <<report_text, include=FALSE>>=
  ## 1. Number of clusters
  
  # CritCF values (rows: imputed data sets, columns: explored k) and explored
  # numbers of clusters.
  crit <- miclust_1$critcf 
ks   <- 2:5 

# Optimal k in each imputed data set (highest CritCF) and number of imputed
# data sets in which k is optimal.
best_k <- ks[apply(crit, 1, which.max)]
n_best <- sum(best_k == kfin)

# Choice of k: no overlap between the CritCF of kfin and
# the other k, kfin optimal in all data sets but with overlap, or kfin not
# optimal in all data sets.
txt_k <- if (min(crit[, ks == kfin]) > max(crit[, ks != kfin])) {
  sprintf("even the worst value of CritCF for $k=%d$ is above the best value for any other $k$, so $k=%d$ was optimal in all the imputed data sets and there is practically no uncertainty in the choice of $k$", kfin, kfin)
} else if (n_best == M) {
  sprintf("$k=%d$ was optimal in all the imputed data sets, although the CritCF distributions of different values of $k$ overlap", kfin)
} else {
  sprintf("$k=%d$ was optimal in %d of the %d imputed data sets, so there is some uncertainty in the choice of $k$", kfin, n_best, M)
}

# Warning if kfin is the largest explored k.
txt_klim <- if (kfin == max(ks)) " Since $k_{fin}$ is at the upper limit of the explored range, larger values of $k$ should also be explored." else ""

## 2. Kappa

# Level of agreement after relabeling (Landis and Koch (1977) scale.)
km <- median(kappa_vals)
txt_kappa <- if (km > 0.8) "almost perfect" else if (km > 0.6) "substantial" else if (km > 0.4) "moderate" else "low"

## 3. Variable selection

# Drop in selection frequency after the last selected variable. We consider a
# drop of 30 points or more a clear separation.
gap   <- perc[var_sel] - perc[var_sel + 1]
txt_var <- if (gap >= 30) {
  sprintf("There is a clear drop in frequency right after the last selected variable (from %s\\%% to %s\\%%), so the variable selection is robust to the missing data.", p_last, p_next) 
} else {
  sprintf("The drop in frequency after the last selected variable is small (from %s\\%% to %s\\%%), so there is some uncertainty in the variable selection.", p_last, p_next)
}

## 4. Stability of the assignment with k = kfin + 1

# The assignment is considered stable if the first quartile of the
# probability of assignment is 1 in all cluster
txt_next <- if (all(a2[, 2] == 1)) {
  sprintf("For $k=%d$ the assignment is also very stable, probably because the selected variables have few missing values. Given this, we see that a stable assignment does not necessarily mean that the clusters are adequate, we have already seen that the choice of $k = %d$ is supported by the CritCF criterion.", kfin + 1, kfin)
} else {
  sprintf("For $k=%d$ the assignment is less stable, which also supports the choice of $k=%d$.", kfin + 1, kfin)
}

## 5. Cluster description

# Cluster with the lowest FEV1.
i_fev <- which(vars == "fevp_postv1")
sev   <- which.min(mean_imputed[i_fev, ])

# Comparison of raw and imputed means.
txt_raw <- if (max(abs(raw_means - mean_imputed) / abs(mean_imputed)) < 0.05) {
  "very similar"
} else {
  "somewhat different"
}
@
  
To find the number of clusters, we applied the backward search with the CritCF criterion to each of the \Sexpr{M} imputed data sets.
Figure~\ref{fig:fig_critcf} shows that \Sexpr{txt_k}.\Sexpr{txt_klim} After the assignation, the clusters contain \Sexpr{n_final[1]} and \Sexpr{n_final[2]} subjects.

To prevent a potential problem caused by the automatic labeling of the clusters, the clusters were relabeled taking the first data set as a reference and evaluating them with Cohen's kappa, same as the article. The agreement after relabeling is \Sexpr{txt_kappa}, with a median Cohen's kappa of \Sexpr{kappa_med}.

For $k=\Sexpr{kfin}$, \Sexpr{var_once} of the \Sexpr{var_cand} candidate variables were selected in at least one data set (Figures~\ref{fig:fig_varsel} and~\ref{fig:fig_barplot}). The final subset has \Sexpr{var_sel} variables, \Sexpr{var_sel_all} of which were selected in all the imputed data sets.
\Sexpr{txt_var}

Each subject was assigned to the cluster to which they were allocated most often across the imputed data sets.  In table ~\ref{tab:alloc} we can see how this frequency is distributed in  $k = \Sexpr{kfin}$ and  $k = \Sexpr{kfin + 1}$. For $k = \Sexpr{kfin}$, the median probability is \Sexpr{med1} in both clusters, so most subjects are always assigned to the same cluster.   \Sexpr{txt_next}

Table~\ref{tab:desc} shows that cluster \Sexpr{sev} groups the subjects with more severe disease, with worse lung function, more air trapping, lower arterial oxygen and worse scores in the questionnaires. We also see that results with raw and imputed data are \Sexpr{txt_raw}.



